+# Specialty-Narrowed Veterinarian Queries Are Not Cached
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The veterinarian repository's two unnarrowed read methods are cached under the `vets` cache. That cache is created through the JCache API with statistics enabled and nothing else set — no size limit, no expiry, no eviction. [system-design Open Questions](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) records that gap.
+
+`REQ-VET-004` adds read methods narrowed by a specialty name taken from a query parameter. Caching them would key entries on a string any anonymous caller chooses, because every route here is open.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the narrowed methods under the existing `vets` cache.** Rejected: the key space becomes whatever callers send, and nothing evicts it.
+2. **Cache them under a new, bounded cache.** Rejected: that needs provider-specific size and expiry settings no cache here carries, for a query over three seeded specialties.
+3. **Leave the narrowed methods uncached** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty-narrowed veterinarian queries carry no cache annotation. The two unnarrowed methods keep theirs, unchanged.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every narrowed request reaches the database. The directory is small, and the join it traverses is keyed on both sides.
+- The `vets` cache gains no new caller-controlled key dimension. Its existing paging key is already caller-controlled — recorded under Open Questions.
+- Two adjacent repository methods now differ in caching. The back-link from [system-design Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) is what explains the asymmetry.
+- Bounding and evicting the `vets` cache stays open. Settling it is the point at which caching the narrowed queries can be revisited.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the current-state rule.
+- [system-design.md Open Questions](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — the unbounded `vets` cache.
